<p>Attacks on energy sites and shipping snarls around the Strait of Hormuz sent oil markets swinging, with reports of tankers unable to move and oil and gas facilities hit across the region. Several outlets said prices briefly surged toward $120 a barrel before easing back—an early reminder of how fast conflict can ripple through global energy supplies. Governments and industry are now looking for stopgap ways to keep fuel moving. G7 officials held crisis talks and signaled that options such as strategic stockpile releases are on the table if conditions worsen. Analysts say the bigger question is what happens if disruption around Hormuz drags on: it could reroute global oil flows and strain the energy system’s ability to absorb shocks.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/IrTlf8G75luhXSA7OB7iuEyPYN24skE11frcn7emLlGoxXEvSWGW2co6SJ1GAhhCY4QeIy2z1JL3srdvepuUNwvVR5guwdabps8DCnDw' alt='Women hold posters of Iran’s new supreme leader, Ayatollah Mojtaba Khamenei, during a rally in Tehran.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Poll signals: A Reuters/Ipsos poll found most Americans expect gasoline prices to rise in the coming months after President Trump’s decision to launch military strikes on Iran, and many expect the conflict to drag on.</li><li>Shipping under fire: At least five oil tankers were reported hit by drones or other projectiles as Iran threatened vessels trying to transit the area.</li><li>Gas supply hit: A top global natural gas exporter in Qatar suspended production after Iran attacked two of its sites, a reminder that the risk extends beyond crude oil.</li><li>Wider regional impacts: Saudi officials said they intercepted drones targeting an oil refinery, while debris from a downed drone sparked a fire at an energy hub in the UAE.</li><li>Iran leadership change: Mojtaba Khamenei was named Iran’s new supreme leader, a move widely read as signaling continuity in hardline policies amid the conflict.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iran foreign ministry: Iran’s foreign minister promised “surprises” and branded the strikes against Iran “Operation Epic Mistake,” as Iran and Israel launched new strikes. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/09/world/middleeast/iran-war-israel-us.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>French finance ministry (G7 chair): France’s finance minister Roland Lescure said the G7 was “not there yet” on releasing strategic reserves, but agreed it could use tools—including potential stockpile releases—to steady markets. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1980433/strategic-oil-reserves-on-the-table-as-g7-meets'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Financial Times analysis: Analysts said a strategic-reserve release may not fix the Gulf’s physical shipping and infrastructure bottlenecks, even if it adds barrels on paper. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/2ac57d2f-213f-42ee-98a9-a1e14e819915'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Market analysts: Analysts warned the conflict could leave lasting geoeconomic scars—reshaping global oil flows and testing the international energy system’s resilience in a decades-style disruption. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/09/2026/analysts-warn-of-iran-wars-lasting-geoeconomic-impacts'>Semafor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-how-long-before-gulf-nations-stop-pumping-oil/a-76272635'>Iran war: How long before Gulf nations stop pumping oil?</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/09/world/middleeast/iran-war-israel-us.html'>Oil Prices and Stocks Swing Wildly as Iran and Israel Launch New Strikes</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/strait-of-hormuz-iran-oil-prices-us/'>Prolonged Strait of Hormuz closure would cause oil prices to surge, experts warn</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/09/2026/analysts-warn-of-iran-wars-lasting-geoeconomic-impacts'>Analysts warn of Iran war&#x27;s lasting geoeconomic impacts</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https:/